'''Prehistoric Southeastern Kaftia''' spans the period from the emergence of anatomically modern humans in the [[Enfolo]] plateau (approximately 300,000 years ago) to the rise of the [[Qush civilization]] in the river valleys of [[Jarin]] around 4000 BCE. The region is the cradle of humanity on [[Adonia]], with Enfolo serving as the evolutionary homeland from which humans dispersed across the entire planet.

== Paleolithic ==

=== Cradle of humanity ===
{{main|Enfolo Plateau}}
The earliest known anatomically modern human remains on the planet [[Adonia]] have been discovered in the '''savanna transition zone''' — the open grasslands stretching across northern [[Enfolo]], northern [[Ayorin]], and southern [[Tamazgha]]. This region, situated between the arid desert to the north and the dense cloud forests of the Enfolo plateau to the south, provided the ideal environment for human evolution: open terrain supporting large herbivore herds, scattered woodlands for shelter, and abundant water sources draining from the highlands.

Fossil discoveries in the savanna transition zone date to approximately 300,000 BP, making this region the evolutionary homeland of all humanity. Key fossil sites include:
* '''Geba Valley''' — the oldest known human fossils on Adonia, dated to ~300,000 BP
* '''Laga Melka shelters''' — rock shelters containing continuous occupation layers from 280,000 to 50,000 BP
* '''Wabi Gorge''' — stratified deposits spanning 200,000 years of human evolution

=== Dispersal routes ===
From the savanna transition zone, human populations dispersed in multiple directions, navigating around the barriers of the [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]] and the expanding [[Tanaruf n-Azref|desert]]:

The '''western dispersal''' followed two routes around the mountains. One stream passed through the [[Ayorin]] rainforest corridor into the northern basin of [[Southwestern Kaftia]], becoming the ancestors of the [[Sarafoni people|Sarafoni]], [[Lebu people|Lebu]], [[Dakari people|Dakari]], and [[Tukulor people|Tukulor]] peoples. This route is equivalent to the "northeastern migration" described in [[Prehistoric Southwestern Kaftia]]. A second stream followed the highland corridors through [[Orma]] into the southern highlands of Southwestern Kaftia, becoming the ancestors of the [[Burawi people|Burawi]] and [[Orma people|Orma]] peoples. This route is equivalent to the "eastern highland migration" described in the Southwestern Kaftian literature.

The '''eastern dispersal''' crossed the [[Desket Escarpment]] to reach the [[Kendriyan Ocean]] coast. Communities established themselves along the coastal plain of [[Desket]] and [[Nabia]] by approximately 150,000 BP, and from there spread northward along the Kendriyan littoral.

The '''northern dispersal''' moved deeper into the expanding [[Tanaruf n-Azref|desert]], adapting to increasingly arid conditions. These populations developed the specialized desert survival techniques that would define the Desert Tradition.

The '''southern dispersal''' followed the river valleys draining from the Enfolo plateau toward the [[Mesogean Sea]]. These populations settled the fertile coastal plains of [[Jarin]], [[Itegar]], and the Mesogean coast of [[Orma]], establishing the earliest Mesogean coast communities by approximately 200,000 BP.

The '''southeastern dispersal''' followed the Mesogean coast through [[Jarin]] to the [[Triphylian Isthmus]]. This narrow land bridge connected Kaftia to [[Illypnia]], and from there, populations spread to all other continents of [[Adonia]]. The Triphylian corridor thus served as the gateway through which humans left the Kaftian continent and populated the rest of the planet.

=== Hilal settlement ===
{{main|Hilal|Hilal Tradition}}
The island of [[Hilal]] presents a unique case in Kaftian prehistory. During glacial periods of the Pleistocene, lower sea levels connected Hilal to the mainland, allowing early human populations to reach the island on foot. The earliest archaeological evidence on Hilal dates to approximately 180,000 BP, representing one of the earliest human settlements outside the Enfolo core.

As the last ice age ended and sea levels rose (~12,000–8,000 BCE), Hilal became separated from the mainland by the [[Strait of Azref]] and [[Strait of Guzo]]. The island's population was isolated, developing distinct cultural and linguistic traditions in seclusion. Later settlement waves reached Hilal by sea, but the initial isolated population formed the foundation of the island's unique cultural character.

== Neolithic ==

The Neolithic period in Southeastern Kaftia saw the development of seven distinct cultural traditions, each adapted to the region's varied environments. Unlike Southwestern Kaftia, where the [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]] divided populations, Southeastern Kaftia's traditions formed around environmental zones radiating from the savanna transition zone — the cradle of humanity.

=== Savanna transition tradition ===
{{main|Savanna Transition Tradition}}
The '''Savanna Transition Tradition''' developed in the open grasslands stretching across northern [[Enfolo]], northern [[Ayorin]], and southern [[Tamazgha]] — the same region where anatomically modern humans first evolved. As the oldest cultural tradition on the continent, it retained many archaic features while developing distinctive adaptations to the savanna environment. The tradition is characterised by:

* '''Persistence hunting''': Endurance-based hunting techniques exploiting human stamina to pursue prey over long distances in open terrain
* '''Savanna pastoralism''': Early domestication of indigenous antelope species and the development of nomadic herding patterns
* '''Open-air settlements''': Seasonal camps following the migration of large herbivore herds
* '''Tumulus burial''': Construction of earthen burial mounds for prominent individuals, a tradition that would later spread to northern Southwestern Kaftia

The Savanna Transition Tradition served as the source population for all dispersal routes, with groups gradually moving outward into adjacent environmental zones while the core population remained in the grasslands.

=== Ayorin tradition ===
{{main|Ayorin Tradition}}
The '''Ayorin Tradition''' developed in the tropical delta and rainforest region of [[Ayorin]], where the [[Amara Delta]] and coastal rainforests provided a distinct environment from the adjacent savanna. The tradition is characterised by:

* '''Delta horticulture''': Flood-recession agriculture in the fertile delta soils, cultivating rice, yams, and aquatic plants
* '''Rainforest foraging''': Specialized gathering of forest products including fruits, nuts, medicinal plants, and honey
* '''Stilt-house settlements''': Elevated dwellings built over waterways and floodplains, providing protection from seasonal flooding
* '''Canoe culture''': Extensive use of dugout canoes for transportation, fishing, and trade along the delta's waterways

The Ayorin Tradition maintained close contact with the Savanna Transition Tradition along the forest-savanna ecotone, facilitating the exchange of crops, technologies, and cultural practices.

=== Enfolo tradition ===
{{main|Enfolo Tradition}}
The '''Enfolo Tradition''' represents the oldest continuous cultural lineage in Kaftia. Communities in the cloud forest highlands retained many archaic technological and social features well into the Neolithic, protected by the plateau's isolation and abundant resources. The tradition is characterised by:

* '''Forest horticulture''': Domestication of indigenous tubers, fruits, and leaf vegetables in forest clearings
* '''Arboreal hunting''': Specialized hunting techniques for forest-dwelling species using blowguns, nets, and traps
* '''Treehouse settlements''': Elevated dwellings constructed in the forest canopy, providing protection from ground-level predators and humidity
* '''Megalithic shrines''': Stone monuments erected in forest clearings, associated with ancestor veneration and forest spirit worship

The Enfolo Tradition remained relatively isolated throughout the prehistoric period, with limited exchange with neighbouring traditions due to the difficulty of travel through dense jungle terrain.

=== Desert tradition ===
{{main|Desert Tradition}}
The '''Desert Tradition''' emerged in [[Tamazgha]] as populations adapted to the increasingly arid conditions of the northern interior. The tradition is characterised by:

* '''Oasis horticulture''': Intensive agriculture around permanent water sources, including date palm cultivation and irrigation systems
* '''Nomadic pastoralism''': Seasonal movement of goat and camel herds across desert grazing lands
* '''Rock art''': Extensive petroglyphs and pictographs depicting desert fauna, hunting scenes, and geometric patterns
* '''Underground dwellings''': Subterranean houses providing insulation from extreme temperatures

The Desert Tradition developed sophisticated water management technologies, including qanat-style underground aqueducts and cistern systems for storing seasonal rainfall.

=== Mesogean coast tradition ===
{{main|Mesogean Coast Tradition (Southeastern Kaftia)}}
The '''Mesogean Coast Tradition''' developed along the southern coast of Southeastern Kaftia, centered on the fertile river valleys of [[Jarin]] and [[Itegar]]. This tradition is the oldest of the coastal cultures and predates the related Mesogean traditions of Southwestern Kaftia by several millennia. The tradition is characterised by:

* '''River valley agriculture''': Flood-recession farming along the [[Gash River|Gash]] and [[Baraka River|Baraka]] rivers, cultivating early forms of wheat, barley, and legumes
* '''Maritime trade''': Coastal navigation and trade with communities along the Mesogean littoral
* '''Permanent settlements''': Large villages and early proto-urban centres along river banks
* '''Pottery tradition''': Distinctive painted ceramics with geometric and figurative designs

The Mesogean Coast Tradition of Southeastern Kaftia had limited contact with its Southwestern counterpart. The mountainous terrain and dense river deltas of the [[Maianka]]–[[Sabia]]–[[Kushish]] region created a natural barrier, restricting exchange to occasional contact through [[Orma]] and highland passes.

=== Kendriyan coast tradition ===
{{main|Kendriyan Coast Tradition}}
The '''Kendriyan Coast Tradition''' developed along the eastern coast facing the [[Kendriyan Ocean]], in the territories of modern [[Desket]] and [[Nabia]]. The tradition is characterised by:

* '''Escarpment adaptation''': Settlements on the plateau summit and coastal plain, with seasonal movement between zones
* '''Deep-sea fishing''': Ocean-going vessels and specialized techniques for pelagic fish species
* '''Shell mound culture''': Massive shell middens serving as territorial markers and burial sites
* '''Stone circle monuments''': Megalithic constructions associated with astronomical observations

The dramatic [[Desket Escarpment]] created two distinct sub-zones within the tradition: the humid plateau communities and the arid coastal plain communities, each with specialized adaptations.

=== Hilal tradition ===
{{main|Hilal Tradition}}
The '''Hilal Tradition''' developed in isolation on [[Hilal]] island following its separation from the mainland. The tradition is characterised by:

* '''Island adaptation''': Unique technologies and social structures developed in isolation
* '''Seabird harvesting''': Sustainable exploitation of the island's massive seabird colonies for food, feathers, and guano
* '''Megalithic architecture''': Distinctive stone monuments found nowhere else in Kaftia
* '''Unique language family''': The [[Hilali languages]] have no known relatives on the mainland

The Hilal Tradition represents a cultural isolate comparable to the Gaelic cultures of remote islands, preserving archaic features and developing unique innovations in seclusion. Limited contact with the mainland occurred through occasional maritime trade, but the island's population remained culturally distinct throughout the prehistoric period.

== Chalcolithic ==

{{W|Copper}} metallurgy appeared in Southeastern Kaftia around 4000 BCE, with the earliest evidence from the [[Gash River]] valley in [[Jarin]]. Copper working emerged independently in the Mesogean coast tradition, possibly stimulated by the increasing complexity of river valley societies.

The Chalcolithic period coincided with the emergence of the [[Qush civilization]] in Jarin, marking the transition from prehistoric to historic times. Copper tools and ornaments became prestige goods among the growing elite of the early Qush city-states, while communities outside the river valleys continued to rely on stone tools.

== Bronze Age ==

The Bronze Age in Southeastern Kaftia was centered on the [[Enfolo]] plateau and the [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] coast, where copper and tin deposits occurred in close proximity. Indigenous bronze production developed around 3000 BCE, though the technology remained concentrated in the highlands and the Jarin river valleys.

Bronze weapons and tools did not spread rapidly to the desert, Kendriyan coast, or Hilal. These regions continued to rely on stone and copper technologies well into the period when bronze was common in the core zones.

== Iron Age ==

{{W|Iron}} working reached Southeastern Kaftia through trade contacts with Southwestern Kaftia around 1000 BCE, where the [[Kingdom of Ufyen]] had developed indigenous iron smelting. Iron technology spread rapidly along the Mesogean coast, reaching [[Jarin]] and [[Itegar]] by 800 BCE.

The adoption of iron working coincided with the mature phase of the Qush civilization, which had already developed complex urban societies, writing systems, and state-level political organization. Iron tools enhanced agricultural productivity and military capacity, but the Qush civilization's core institutions predated the technology.

== Linguistic history ==

The linguistic landscape of Southeastern Kaftia reflects the region's role as the cradle of humanity and the subsequent dispersal of populations from the savanna transition zone.

=== Savanna languages ===
{{main|Savanna languages}}
The languages of the savanna transition zone represent the deepest linguistic lineages on the continent. The Savanna language family shows extreme internal diversity, consistent with hundreds of thousands of years of development in situ. These languages are characterised by complex tonal systems, extensive noun class systems, and rich vocabularies related to grazing animals, seasonal patterns, and open-terrain hunting.

=== Ayorin languages ===
{{main|Ayorin languages}}
The languages of the [[Ayorin]] delta and rainforest belong to a distinct family that diverged early from the Savanna family as populations moved into the forested environment. Ayorin languages are characterised by noun class systems adapted to riverine and forest ecology, and vocabularies rich in terms for watercraft, fishing, and tropical forest flora.

=== Enfolan languages ===
{{main|Enfolan languages}}
The languages of the [[Enfolo]] plateau represent a branch of the Savanna family that adapted to the highland cloud forest environment. Enfolan languages show the influence of isolation in the dense forest, with simplified tonal systems compared to the Savanna languages but expanded vocabularies for forest ecology.

=== Qushic languages ===
{{main|Qushic languages}}
The languages of the [[Jarin]] river valleys, including the language of the [[Qush civilization]], belong to the Qushic family. These languages show affinity with the Enfolan family but diverged early in the southern dispersal. Qushic languages are characterised by {{W|subject–object–verb}} word order and agglutinative morphology.

=== Hilali languages ===
{{main|Hilali languages}}
The [[Hilali languages]] are a linguistic isolate with no known relatives on the mainland. The language family developed in isolation on [[Hilal]] island following its separation from the mainland, preserving archaic features lost elsewhere and developing unique innovations. The relationship between Hilali and the mainland families remains a subject of scholarly debate.

=== Desert languages ===
{{main|Desert languages}}
The languages of [[Tamazgha]] belong to multiple small families and isolates, reflecting the fragmented nature of desert populations. These languages show influences from both Enfolan and northern Kaftian languages, consistent with the region's position as a crossroads.

== Genetic history ==

Genetic studies of ancient and modern populations in Southeastern Kaftia confirm the region's status as the cradle of humanity on the planet.

=== Savanna transition populations ===
The peoples of the savanna transition zone — spanning northern [[Enfolo]], northern [[Ayorin]], and southern [[Tamazgha]] — show the highest genetic diversity on the planet, consistent with the region's role as the source population for all humanity. Savanna populations carry the deepest mitochondrial and Y-chromosome lineages, representing the ancestral stock from which all other populations diverged.

=== Ayorin populations ===
The peoples of the [[Ayorin]] delta and rainforest show a distinct genetic cluster reflecting early settlement from the adjacent savanna. The transition from savanna to forest environment created a genetic bottleneck, with Ayorin populations showing reduced diversity compared to the source population but higher diversity than more distant dispersal populations.

=== Enfolo populations ===
The peoples of the [[Enfolo]] plateau show a genetic profile consistent with early settlement from the northern savanna fringe, followed by prolonged isolation in the cloud forest environment. Enfolan populations retain high genetic diversity but show distinctive markers associated with adaptation to highland and forest environments.

=== Dispersal populations ===
Populations outside the savanna transition zone show a pattern of decreasing genetic diversity with distance from the source region, consistent with serial founder effects during the initial dispersals. The [[Hilal]] island population shows the most pronounced founder effect, with reduced genetic diversity consistent with a small founding population isolated for approximately 10,000 years.

=== Mesogean coast populations ===
The populations of [[Jarin]], [[Itegar]], and [[Orma]] show a distinct genetic cluster reflecting early settlement from the savanna transition zone via the southern dispersal route. These populations show evidence of limited admixture with populations from Southwestern Kaftia, primarily through contact in the Orma highlands.

